# THE CLEAN-ROOM HANDOFF PACKAGE. ONE FILE, AND IT IS THE ONLY DEFINITION.
#
# Build it:      ./bin/build-handoff.sh --out <dir>
# Verify it:     ./bin/check-handoff-package.sh
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The package used to be defined by exclusion, inside `bin/check-links.sh`'s
# `DOCS` array. That meant the only thing that knew what shipped was the gate
# checking it: nothing could assemble the package, and nothing could ask what
# was in it. This file is that definition lifted out to where a builder, a gate
# and a reader can all read the same answer — the same shape as `.tool-versions`,
# which made one manifest the only place the toolchain is named.
#
# ENTRY KINDS
#   file <path>            one file, repo-relative
#   tree <dir> <pattern>   every `find <dir> -name <pattern> -type f` match
#
# The `tree` entries are globs rather than a list on purpose. A feature file
# added tomorrow ships tomorrow, with nobody remembering to come here — and
# `check-handoff-package.sh` check 1 fails the build if the trees below ever
# stop covering what is on disk, so an omission cannot pass quietly.

# --- The specification a recipient implements from -------------------------
# `README.md` is first because it is literally the first file anyone opens.
file README.md
file LANGUAGE.md
file TOUR.md
file CONTEXT.md
file PRELUDE.md

# --- The compiler's own documentation --------------------------------------
# Not the compiler SOURCE. A clean-room recipient implements the compiler from
# this package; shipping an implementation would defeat the exercise. What ships
# is the record of what was built and why.
file compiler/README.md
tree compiler/features *.md

# --- The corpus, which is the specification's oracle ------------------------
# Every example opens with the command that runs it. These are what a recipient
# checks an implementation against, so they ship whole.
#
# `*` RATHER THAN `*.bs` AND `*.md`, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION. The first draft
# named those two extensions and silently dropped
# `compiler/examples/exemplars/FRONTIER` — a 44-line data file recording where
# each exemplar stops, which `exemplars/README.md` links to TWICE. The gate
# caught it as a dangling link, which is the right way round, but the cause was
# a manifest that described the corpus by file type instead of by content.
# Everything in this tree is package material; enumerate it that way.
tree compiler/examples *
